'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Iberian ham works well as a gift because it combines gastronomy, celebration and a strong sense of occasion. But the best choice is not always the largest or most expensive piece. <strong>Format, household size, carving experience and budget</strong> can matter just as much as the legal category of the ham.</p>
<p>Before buying, make four decisions: ham or shoulder, whole piece or sliced packs, which Iberian category fits the budget, and what size is practical.</p>
<h2>Whole ham: the most impressive gift</h2>
<p>A whole ham has the greatest visual impact. It suits families, frequent gatherings and people who enjoy carving and eating a piece over several weeks. Its drawback is practical: it requires a stand, a suitable knife and reasonably regular consumption.</p>
<p>If the recipient lives alone, rarely eats ham or does not want to carve it, a whole leg may be less convenient than it first appears.</p>
<h2>Iberian shoulder: lower spend, plenty of character</h2>
<p>A shoulder comes from the front leg and is smaller than a ham. It can deliver intense flavour and the ritual of a whole piece for a lower total price. It also gets finished sooner, which can be useful in smaller households.</p>
<p>Its edible yield is lower because it contains proportionally more bone. That is why total purchase price should not be the only comparison. See our guide to <a href="/en/ham-or-shoulder-differences-how-to-choose/">ham versus shoulder</a>.</p>
<h2>Sliced packs: often the easiest gift to get right</h2>
<p>Vacuum-packed slices remove most of the inconvenience. No carving skill is needed, only the required amount is opened and storage is simple. They are ideal for couples, smaller households, older recipients and corporate gifts.</p>
<p>This format can also let you spend more of the budget on a higher product category rather than on the size of a whole piece.</p>
<h2>Acorn-fed, field-fed or feed-fed?</h2>
<p><strong>Bellota</strong> refers to Iberian pigs finished during the montanera under the regulated system. <strong>Cebo de campo</strong> combines feed with an outdoor production system, while <strong>cebo</strong> refers to animals fed with formulated feeds under authorised intensive conditions.</p>
<p>Bellota normally communicates the most premium gift. A good cebo de campo product can make excellent sense when budget matters. Read our complete guide to <a href="/en/acorn-fed-field-fed-feed-fed-iberian-ham-differences/">bellota, cebo de campo and cebo</a>.</p>
<h2>50%, 75% or 100% Iberian is a different question</h2>
<p>The Iberian percentage refers to <strong>breed</strong>, not feeding. A product can therefore be acorn-fed and 50% Iberian, or acorn-fed and 100% Iberian. These are separate parts of the legal description.</p>
<p>For the most recognisable premium gift, 100% Iberian acorn-fed ham sits at the top of the regulated categories. Other combinations can offer excellent value.</p>
<h2>What weight should you choose?</h2>
<p>A heavier ham is not automatically better. Weight determines how much product the household needs to finish and affects how practical the gift will be. The stated weight includes bone, rind and external fat, so the amount of sliced meat is substantially lower.</p>
<h2>Quick recommendations by recipient</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Large family:</strong> whole ham if they can carve it.</li>
<li><strong>Couple or small household:</strong> shoulder or sliced packs.</li>
<li><strong>Iberian-ham enthusiast:</strong> a clearly labelled higher-category whole piece.</li>
<li><strong>Convenience first:</strong> vacuum-packed slices.</li>
<li><strong>Corporate gift:</strong> easy-to-store sliced formats.</li>
<li><strong>Major celebration:</strong> bellota, with a higher breed percentage if the budget allows.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What to check before buying</h2>
<p>Look at the complete legal sales description, breed percentage where applicable, colour seal, weight and producer. Do not choose solely because of marketing terms such as “reserve” or “special selection” if the regulated information is unclear.</p>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/en/black-red-green-white-seal-iberian-ham/">black, red, green and white seals</a> explains the official categories.</p>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>A whole ham creates the biggest occasion. A shoulder gives a whole-piece experience at a lower total spend. Sliced packs are usually the safest choice when convenience matters. Once format is decided, choose feeding category, Iberian percentage and weight according to the importance of the gift and the recipient.</p>
